# RFC-1024: Deterministic Dependency Graph Pruning and Ephemeral MicroVM Orchestration Protocol (DDGP-EMOP) for AI-Generated Code-Commit Pipelines

## Section 1: RFC Scope, Terminology & Guarantees

### 1.1 Context & Problem Statement
With the proliferation of autonomous AI coding agents (such as Devin, Muse, and internal multi-agent code-generation loops), software engineering organizations are experiencing an unprecedented surge in code-commit frequencies [1]. While human developers commit code several times a day, cooperative AI agent swarms can generate, test, refactor, and commit code at rates exceeding hundreds of commits per hour per repository. 

Traditional Continuous Integration (CI) systems—designed for human-scale cadences—rely on heavyweight containerization, linear queueing, and coarse-grained test execution. Under AI agent workloads, these systems suffer from severe queue congestion, resource exhaustion, and prohibitive compute costs. To maintain a non-blocking development loop, CI/CD infrastructure must evolve to execute tests with microsecond-level scheduling latency, absolute hardware-level isolation, and deterministic test-suite pruning.

This document specifies **RFC-1024**, which defines the **Deterministic Dependency Graph Pruning (DDGP)** algorithm and the **Ephemeral MicroVM Orchestration Protocol (EMOP)**. Together, they establish a high-throughput, low-latency execution fabric designed specifically for high-frequency, AI-generated code pipelines.

```
                               +----------------------------------+
                               |  AI Agent Commit Stream (100Hz)  |
                               +-----------------+----------------+
                                                 | 
                                                 v
                               +-----------------+----------------+
                               |   DDGP Engine: Lock-Free DAG     |
                               |   Incremental Graph Pruner       |
                               +-----------------+----------------+
                                                 | 
                                                 | (Pruned Test Targets)
                                                 v
                               +-----------------+----------------+
                               |  EMOP Orchestrator: Ephemeral    |
                               |  Firecracker MicroVM Pool        |
                               +--------+----------------+--------+
                                        |                |
                     +------------------v---+        +---v------------------+
                     | MicroVM Instance #1  |  ...   | MicroVM Instance #N  |
                     | (TAP, Read-Only Root)|        | (TAP, Read-Only Root)|
                     +----------------------+        +----------------------+
```

### 1.2 Terminology
* **DAG (Directed Acyclic Graph):** The representation of project targets and their compilation/test dependencies.
* **DDGP (Deterministic Dependency Graph Pruning):** An incremental, lock-free graph traversal algorithm that identifies the minimal subset of test targets affected by a given code mutation.
* **EMOP (Ephemeral MicroVM Orchestration Protocol):** A lightweight control-plane protocol that provisions, executes, and tears down hardware-isolated microVMs via Unix domain sockets in sub-10ms cycles.
* **MicroVM (Micro Virtual Machine):** A minimalist virtual machine running on a hypervisor (e.g., Firecracker) utilizing KVM (Kernel-based Virtual Machine) for hardware-assisted virtualization [2].
* **TAP Device:** A virtual network kernel interface that simulates a link-layer device, used here to provide isolated, high-performance networking to MicroVMs.

### 1.3 Protocol Guarantees
1. **Deterministic Minimality:** For any commit $C_i$ mutating source files $F$, the set of executed tests $T_e$ is strictly bounded by the transitive closure of the dependency paths originating from $F$. No unaffected tests shall be executed.
2. **Hardware-Level Isolation:** Every test suite run executes inside an isolated address space with dedicated virtual CPUs (vCPUs), memory, and a read-only root filesystem with copy-on-write (CoW) overlay block devices.
3. **Microsecond-Scale Scheduling Overhead:** The latency from the arrival of a commit event to the boot-up of a dedicated MicroVM and the initiation of the test runner must remain below $10\\text{ milliseconds}$.
4. **Zero-Leak State Invariant:** No state, mutation, or memory artifact from a test run can persist to pollute subsequent runs. All execution environments are discarded post-execution.

