# RFC-0312: Zero-Loss Asynchronous Agent Tool Execution Protocol (ATEP)

**Status:** Standards Track  
**Category:** Protocol Specification & Systems Architecture  
**Authors:** LoLaBo Systems Architecture Working Group  
**Date:** March 2026  

---

## Section 1: RFC Scope, Terminology & Guarantees

Modern agentic workflows rely heavily on distributed, asynchronous tool invocation pipelines (e.g., model context protocol dispatch, third-party API batching, code execution sandboxes). A critical vulnerability in high-throughput asynchronous execution fabrics is the **silent state dropped job anomaly** [1]. When upstream Large Language Model (LLM) agents issue batch tool execution calls over ephemeral HTTP/2 or WebSockets, transient worker drops, network partition boundaries, or unacknowledged socket disconnects frequently result in execution requests lingering in unmonitored `PENDING` states without error logging, feedback, or state reconciliation.

`RFC-0312` introduces the **Asynchronous Agent Tool Execution Protocol (ATEP)**. ATEP is an application-layer wire protocol and runtime state engine designed to enforce zero-loss execution guarantees, strict state idempotency, and sub-millisecond failure detection across distributed execution boundaries.

```
+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|                                 ATEP ARCHITECTURE TOPOLOGY                            |
|                                                                                       |
|   +-------------------+      Zero-Copy      +-------------------------------------+   |
|   |  Agent Controller | ------------------> |    ATEP Intent Ring-Buffer (SPSC)   |   |
|   |  (Task Producer)  |   Intent Frame      |  64-Byte Cache-Aligned Lock-Free Queue|   |
|   +-------------------+                     +-------------------------------------+   |
|             |                                                  |                      |
|      Heartbeat Probe                                    WAL Ring Persist              |
|             v                                                  v                      |
|   +-------------------+                     +-------------------------------------+   |
|   | State Monitor &   | <--- ACK/NACK ----- |    Asynchronous Worker Execution    |   |
|   | Replay Controller |      Heartbeat      |    Engine (Tool Sandbox / Engine)    |   |
|   +-------------------+                     +-------------------------------------+   |
+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
```

### Terminology
* **ATEP Agent Frame (`AFrame`):** A fixed-header, 128-byte alignment-optimized binary packet carrying intent metadata, execution payload offset, routing keys, and cryptographically verified nonces.
* **Intent Ring Buffer (IRB):** A lock-free, single-producer single-consumer (SPSC) or multi-producer multi-consumer (MPMC) circular memory buffer bound to worker threads, utilizing atomic memory barriers (`Acquire`/`Release`) [3].
* **Write-Ahead Intent Log (WAIL):** A persistent memory-mapped sequence log backed by `io_uring` direct disk offset writes for durability prior to thread dispatch [1].
* **Orphan Execution Horizon (OEH):** The configurable latency threshold ($T_{OEH}$, default: $1500\text{ms}$) after which an unacknowledged tool call execution frame is classified as lost, triggering deterministic replay or fallback compensation.

### Guarantees & Invariants
1. **Zero Silent Drops (Invariant 1):** Every intent written to an IRB MUST be recorded to the WAIL before dispatch. A task cannot transition to an unmonitored state; it is either completed (`ACK`), explicitly rejected (`NACK`), or force-replayed (`REPLAY`).
2. **Strict Idempotency Isolation (Invariant 2):** Every `AFrame` carries a 128-bit monotonically increasing execution nonce combined with a payload Blake3 hash. Replayed jobs duplicate the execution key, ensuring side-effect execution enclaves enforce exactly-once state mutation.
3. **Cache-Line Alignment (Invariant 3):** Ring-buffer structures and atomic header positions strictly isolate producer write indices and consumer read indices into distinct 64-byte L1 cache lines, eliminating cross-core false sharing [3].

