## 3. Data Plane vs. Control Plane Protocol Dynamics

### The Control Plane: State Orchestration and Snapshot Provisioning
The control plane operates as a distributed Raft consensus cluster that manages benchmark execution graphs, environment manifests, and validation budgets. When an evaluation rollout begins, the scheduler dispatches a deployment descriptor to localized worker daemons. The worker does not initialize an OS boot sequence; instead, it issues a shallow clone of an existing, memory-mapped guest snapshot via Linux KVM ioctls.

```rust
// Rust Snippet: Low-overhead snapshot cloning via KVM and userfaultfd
use kvm_ioctls::{Kvm, VmFd, VcpuFd};
use kvm_bindings::*;
use std::os::unix::io::AsRawFd;

pub struct MicroVMSnapshotEngine {
    kvm: Kvm,
    vm_fd: VmFd,
    uffd_handle: libc::c_int,
    guest_mem_base: *mut libc::c_void,
    mem_size_bytes: usize,
}

impl MicroVMSnapshotEngine {
    pub unsafe fn branch_execution_context(&self) -> Result<VmFd, Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {
        let cloned_vm = self.kvm.create_vm()?;

        // Map anonymous memory for CoW shadow layer
        let shadow_mem = libc::mmap(
            std::ptr::null_mut(),
            self.mem_size_bytes,
            libc::PROT_READ | libc::PROT_WRITE,
            libc::MAP_PRIVATE | libc::MAP_ANONYMOUS | libc::MAP_NORESERVE,
            -1,
            0,
        );
        if shadow_mem == libc::MAP_FAILED {
            return Err("mmap allocation failed for guest shadow space".into());
        }

        // Register memory region with KVM host
        let mem_region = kvm_userspace_memory_region {
            slot: 0,
            flags: KVM_MEM_LOG_DIRTY_PAGES,
            guest_phys_addr: 0x0,
            memory_size: self.mem_size_bytes as u64,
            userspace_addr: shadow_mem as u64,
        };
        cloned_vm.set_user_memory_region(mem_region)?;

        // Configure userfaultfd to intercept missing page accesses from base snapshot
        let mut uffd: libc::c_int = 0;
        let res = libc::syscall(libc::SYS_userfaultfd, libc::O_CLOEXEC | libc::O_NONBLOCK);
        if res < 0 {
            return Err("Failed to acquire userfaultfd descriptor".into());
        }
        uffd = res as libc::c_int;

        let mut uffd_api = uffdio_api {
            api: UFFD_API,
            features: 0,
            ioctls: 0,
        };
        if libc::ioctl(uffd, UFFDIO_API, &mut uffd_api) < 0 {
            return Err("UFFDIO_API handshake failed".into());
        }

        let mut uffd_register = uffdio_register {
            range: uffdio_range {
                start: shadow_mem as u64,
                len: self.mem_size_bytes as u64,
            },
            mode: UFFDIO_REGISTER_MODE_MISSING,
            ioctls: 0,
        };
        if libc::ioctl(uffd, UFFDIO_REGISTER, &mut uffd_register) < 0 {
            return Err("UFFDIO_REGISTER failed on shadow region".into());
        }

        Ok(cloned_vm)
    }
}
```

When a guest execution core accesses an untouched memory page, the host kernel triggers a page fault intercepted by the user-space `userfaultfd` polling thread. The thread resolves the fault by issue-copying (`UFFDIO_COPY`) exclusively the faulted $4\text{ KB}$ or $2\text{ MB}$ page from the base snapshot backing store, delivering dynamic demand-paging with negligible latency overhead.

### The Data Plane: Flat-Buffered Ring Streaming
During multi-turn execution, agents output high-bandwidth token trajectories, function invocations, and operating system calls. To decouple execution speed from evaluation analysis, the microVM writes directly to an inter-VM shared memory channel (`ivshmem`), bypassing guest TCP network stacks entirely.

```
+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|                         TELEMETRY FRAME FORMAT (64-BYTE ALIGNED)              |
|  +-------------------+-------------------+-------------------+-------------+  |
|  | Magic (32b)       | Sequence ID (64b) | Timestamp (64b)   | PayloadType |  |
|  +-------------------+-------------------+-------------------+-------------+  |
|  | Execution Epoch   | Source Node ID    | Checksum (CRC32C) | Flags       |
|  +-------------------+-------------------+-------------------+-------------+  |
|  |                       Zero-Copy FlatBuffers Payload                       |  |
|  |       (Tokens, Attention Maps, Syscall Registers, State Deltas)           |  |
|  +-------------------------------------------------------------------------+  |
+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
```

```rust
// Rust Snippet: Lock-Free Shared-Memory Ring Buffer Producer
use std::sync::atomic::{AtomicU64, Ordering};

#[repr(C, align(64))]
pub struct SharedRingHeader {
    pub head: AtomicU64,
    pub tail: AtomicU64,
    pub capacity: u64,
    pub mask: u64,
}

#[repr(C, align(64))]
pub struct TelemetrySlot {
    pub sequence: u64,
    pub timestamp_ns: u64,
    pub payload_len: u32,
    pub reserved: u32,
    pub payload: [u8; 4080],
}

pub struct ShmRingProducer {
    header: *mut SharedRingHeader,
    buffer: *mut TelemetrySlot,
}

impl ShmRingProducer {
    pub unsafe fn push(&self, data: &[u8]) -> Result<(), &'static str> {
        if data.len() > 4080 {
            return Err("Payload exceeds slot threshold");
        }

        let header = &*self.header;
        let current_tail = header.tail.load(Ordering::Relaxed);
        let current_head = header.head.load(Ordering::Acquire);

        // Buffer full check
        if (current_tail.wrapping_sub(current_head)) >= header.capacity {
            return Err("Ring buffer saturated");
        }

        let slot_idx = (current_tail & header.mask) as usize;
        let slot = &mut *self.buffer.add(slot_idx);

        // Populate payload with compiler fences ensuring visibility
        slot.sequence = current_tail;
        slot.timestamp_ns = std::time::SystemTime::now()
            .duration_since(std::time::UNIX_EPOCH)
            .unwrap()
            .as_nanos() as u64;
        slot.payload_len = data.len() as u32;

        std::ptr::copy_nonoverlapping(data.as_ptr(), slot.payload.as_mut_ptr(), data.len());

        // Release tail index to consumer
        header.tail.store(current_tail.wrapping_add(1), Ordering::Release);
        Ok(())
    }
}
```

This lock-free design delivers zero-allocation telemetry streaming at host PCIe bus saturation boundaries, fully shielding the guest agent rollout from observability degradation.

