## Section 3: Annotated Code Deconstruction

The core of the DRSE relies on two concurrent sub-routines: a lock-free shadow inspection ring buffer and a transactional atomic commit/rollback engine.

### Code Block 1: Lock-Free Shadow Memory Inspection Ring Buffer

This implementation uses atomic read/write pointers over a fixed-capacity ring buffer, allowing high-throughput decision stream ingress without lock contention.

```rust
use std::sync::atomic::{AtomicU64, AtomicU32, Ordering};
use std::sync::Arc;

pub const RING_BUFFER_CAPACITY: usize = 1024;
pub const STATE_PENDING: u32 = 0;
pub const STATE_VALIDATED: u32 = 1;
pub const STATE_REJECTED: u32 = 2;

#[repr(C, align(64))]
pub struct ShadowSlot {
    pub epoch: AtomicU64,
    pub target_addr: AtomicU64,
    pub expected_val: AtomicU64,
    pub proposed_val: AtomicU64,
    pub status: AtomicU32,
}

pub struct ShadowStateRing {
    pub slots: Box<[ShadowSlot; RING_BUFFER_CAPACITY]>,
    pub head: AtomicU64,
    pub tail: AtomicU64,
}

impl ShadowStateRing {
    pub fn new() -> Self {
        let slots = unsafe { Box::new_zeroed().assume_init() };
        Self {
            slots,
            head: AtomicU64::new(0),
            tail: AtomicU64::new(0),
        }
    }

    #[inline(always)]
    pub fn enqueue_speculative_state(
        &self,
        epoch: u64,
        addr: u64,
        expected: u64,
        proposed: u64,
    ) -> Result<usize, &'static str> {
        let current_tail = self.tail.load(Ordering::Relaxed);
        let current_head = self.head.load(Ordering::Acquire);

        if current_tail - current_head >= RING_BUFFER_CAPACITY as u64 {
            return Err("ERR_RING_BUFFER_FULL");
        }

        let index = (current_tail as usize) % RING_BUFFER_CAPACITY;
        let slot = &self.slots[index];

        slot.epoch.store(epoch, Ordering::Relaxed);
        slot.target_addr.store(addr, Ordering::Relaxed);
        slot.expected_val.store(expected, Ordering::Relaxed);
        slot.proposed_val.store(proposed, Ordering::Relaxed);
        slot.status.store(STATE_PENDING, Ordering::Release);

        self.tail.store(current_tail + 1, Ordering::Release);
        Ok(index)
    }
}
```

#### Detailed Technical Explanation: Code Block 1
1. **`#[repr(C, align(64))]` Padding**: Ensures every slot occupies exact cache line bounds, preventing false sharing across threads concurrently enqueuing decision updates.
2. **`Ordering::Relaxed` vs `Ordering::Release`**: Relaxed memory ordering is used for internal slot writes (`epoch`, `target_addr`, `proposed_val`) to avoid bus locking instructions. The final update to `slot.status` and `self.tail` uses `Ordering::Release`, establishing a happen-before memory synchronization fence for consumer validation workers.
3. **Zero-Heap Allocation Mechanics**: The ring buffer initializes its fixed array upfront. The enqueue path performs pure offset arithmetic without dynamic memory allocations (`malloc`), eliminating garbage collection pause vectors.

