### Hardware Exception Management & Fault Isolation

Because PKU access violations do not invoke standard virtual memory paging faults (they occur in userspace before MMU page table traversal completes), the CPU handles a PKU violation by raising a hardware `#PF` exception with bit 5 (`PF_PK`) set in the error code. The kernel translates this into a synchronous `SIGSEGV` signal sent to the offending process.

To prevent an untrusted agent context from crashing the host supervisor, the control plane registers a dedicated `sigaction` handler configured with `SA_SIGINFO` on an isolated signal stack (`sigaltstack`).

```c
// SIGSEGV Signal Handler for Protection Key Violations
void pku_fault_handler(int sig, siginfo_t *info, void *ctx) {
    if (sig == SIGSEGV && info->si_code == SEGV_PKUERR) {
        // Retrieve the offending key from signal metadata
        int faulting_key = info->si_pkey;

        // Print diagnostic logging out-of-band
        dprintf(STDERR_FILENO, 
            "[CRITICAL] Hardware Sandbox Access Fault! Violation on PKU Domain: %d | Faulting Addr: %p\n",
            faulting_key, info->si_addr);

        // Instantly revoke write access and isolate domain by updating thread PKRU
        // Disable access to all untrusted keys (bits 2..31 set to 0b11)
        uint32_t quarantine_pkru = 0xFFFFFFFC; 
        __wrpkru(quarantine_pkru);

        // Longjmp or terminate execution context gracefully without killing supervisor loop
        _exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }
}

void setup_fault_isolation(void) {
    stack_t ss = {
        .ss_sp = malloc(SIGSTKSZ),
        .ss_size = SIGSTKSZ,
        .ss_flags = 0
    };
    if (sigaltstack(&ss, NULL) == -1) {
        perror("sigaltstack failed");
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }

    struct sigaction sa;
    sa.sa_sigaction = pku_fault_handler;
    sa.sa_flags = SA_SIGINFO | SA_ONSTACK | SA_NODEFER;
    sigemptyset(&sa.sa_mask);

    if (sigaction(SIGSEGV, &sa, NULL) == -1) {
        perror("sigaction registration failed");
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }
}
```

