### Real-Time Syscall Interception via Seccomp-eBPF

To satisfy the **Zero-Kernel Trap Invariant** during normal processing while maintaining structural safety, system calls made by the agent are monitored using a lightweight `seccomp` filter coupled with `eBPF` programs (`BPF_PROG_TYPE_SECCOMP` or tracepoint-attached `BPF_MAP_TYPE_RINGBUF`). The seccomp filter evaluates syscall numbers using a strict whitelist. Attempted unauthorized system calls are blocked instantaneously via `SECCOMP_RET_ERRNO`, entirely bypassing expensive kernel context switching overhead.

```
+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|                             Agent User Domain (PK1)                           |
|  +---------------------+                       +---------------------------+  |
|  | Untrusted Agent Code|                       | Lock-Free SPSC Memory Ring|  |
|  +----------+----------+                       +-------------+-------------+  |
+-------------|------------------------------------------------|----------------+
              | Syscall Attempt                                | Direct Inter-Process
              v                                                | Shared Ring Comm
+------------------------------------------+                   |
| Hardware Enforcement Boundary (MMU / PKU)|                   v
+-------------+----------------------------+     +------------------------------+
              |                                  | Supervisor Control Domain    |
              v                                  | (PK0 / Trusted Host Engine)  |
+------------------------------------------+     +------------------------------+
| Kernel / eBPF Engine Filter              |
| - Whitelist Evaluation                   |
| - BPF Ring Buffer Telemetry Ring         |
+------------------------------------------+
```

```c
// BPF Syscall Monitoring Engine (Kernel Space Telemetry Pipeline)
#include <vmlinux.h>
#include <bpf/bpf_helpers.h>
#include <bpf/bpf_tracing.h>

struct sys_event {
    u32 pid;
    u32 syscall_nr;
    u64 timestamp;
};

struct {
    __uint(type, BPF_MAP_TYPE_RINGBUF);
    __uint(max_entries, 256 * 1024); // 256 KB ring buffer
} syscall_telemetry SEC(".maps");

SEC("tp/raw_syscalls/sys_enter")
int trace_agent_syscall(struct trace_event_raw_sys_enter *ctx) {
    u64 id = bpf_get_current_pid_tgid();
    u32 pid = id >> 32;

    // Filter by specific sub-agent process domain boundary
    // (Assume target_pid is populated by BPF map dynamic lookup)
    struct sys_event *event;

    event = bpf_ringbuf_reserve(&syscall_telemetry, sizeof(*event), 0);
    if (!event) {
        return 0; // OOM on Ring Buffer
    }

    event->pid = pid;
    event->syscall_nr = ctx->id;
    event->timestamp = bpf_ktime_get_ns();

    bpf_ringbuf_submit(event, 0);
    return 0;
}

char _license[] SEC("license") = "GPL";
```

