## Section 2: Low-Level Mechanics & Memory Layout

At the core of a high-performance local memory inspector lies a single-producer, single-consumer (SPSC) lock-free ring buffer backed by shared memory (`shm_open` and `mmap`) to eliminate kernel transitions during agent-to-inspector communication. Traditional mutex-based IPC introduces unacceptable latency spikes ($>15\mu\text{s}$) under high-frequency token generation loads. By utilizing atomic memory orderings (`std::sync::atomic` in Rust or C++11 atomics), we achieve sub-microsecond message exchange.

### Memory Layout & Cache Line Alignment
To prevent cache line bouncing (false sharing) on modern x86_64 and ARM64 architectures, ring buffer metadata indices (`head` and `tail`) are explicitly padded to 64-byte cache line boundaries:

```rust
use std::sync::atomic::{AtomicUsize, Ordering};

#[repr(C, align(64))]
pub struct AlignedAtomicIndex {
    value: AtomicUsize,
}

#[repr(C)]
pub struct SPSCMessageRing<T, const CAP: usize> {
    head: AlignedAtomicIndex,
    tail: AlignedAtomicIndex,
    buffer: [T; CAP],
}
```

By ensuring that `head` and `tail` reside on distinct cache lines, CPU cores do not invalidate each other's L1/L2 caches during concurrent reads and writes. Messages written into the buffer bypass heap allocations entirely; they utilize fixed-size byte arrays structured for zero-copy deserialization.


