### Operational Invariant II: AST-Driven Canonical State Verification

To eliminate the "vibe-coding" trap—where agents continuously overwrite codebases due to superficial formatting variations, comment placements, or import reordering—the state fabric must enforce **AST-Canonical Hashing**. 

The state transition validation pipeline must construct a deterministic Abstract Syntax Tree (AST) representation, strip it of all non-semantic trivia (whitespace, comments, formatting), sort declarations and imports alphabetically, and hash the resulting canonical structure.

```
+------------------+     +------------------+     +-----------------------+
|  Raw Agent Code  | --> | Parser / Lexer   | --> | Strip Non-Semantics   |
|  (With Comments) |     | (e.g., syn/swc)  |     | (Whitespace, Trivia)  |
+------------------+     +------------------+     +-----------------------+
                                                              |
                                                              v
+------------------+     +------------------+     +-----------------------+
| Cryptographic    | <-- | Canonical JSON   | <-- | Sort Declarations     |
| State Hash       |     | Serialization    |     | Deterministically     |
+------------------+     +------------------+     +-----------------------+
```

Below is an implementation of an AST-driven canonical hashing engine using Rust's `syn` and `quote` ecosystems. This module ensures that two code blocks with identical logic but different formatting resolve to the exact same cryptographic state hash.

```rust
use syn::{visit::Visit, File, Item};
use sha2::{Sha256, Digest};
use std::collections::BTreeSet;

/// Traverses a Rust AST and strips non-semantic elements, sorting items
/// deterministically to generate a canonical structural representation.
pub struct CanonicalASTHasher {
    items: BTreeSet<String>,
}

impl<'ast> Visit<'ast> for CanonicalASTHasher {
    fn visit_item(&mut self, node: &'ast Item) {
        // Convert the individual AST node back to a token stream without comments or formatting
        let token_stream = quote::quote!(#node);
        // Normalize spacing by converting the token stream to a string
        let canonical_string = token_stream.to_string();

        // Insert into a BTreeSet to enforce deterministic, alphabetical ordering of items
        // (e.g., sorting functions, structs, and imports regardless of their file position)
        self.items.insert(canonical_string);
    }
}

impl CanonicalASTHasher {
    pub fn new() -> Self {
        Self {
            items: BTreeSet::new(),
        }
    }

    /// Parses raw source code, canonicalizes the AST, and returns a SHA-256 state hash.
    pub fn hash_source(source_code: &str) -> Result<String, syn::Error> {
        let ast: File = syn::parse_str(source_code)?;
        let mut hasher_visitor = Self::new();

        // Populate the BTreeSet with canonicalized AST items
        hasher_visitor.visit_file(&ast);

        // Concatenate the sorted, canonical items into a single byte stream
        let mut sha256 = Sha256::new();
        for item in &hasher_visitor.items {
            sha256.update(item.as_bytes());
        }

        Ok(format!("{:x}", sha256.finalize()))
    }
}

#[cfg(test)]
mod tests {
    use super::*;

    #[test]
    fn test_formatting_insensitivity() {
        let code_a = r#"
            fn calculate_sum(a: i32, b: i32) -> i32 {
                // This is a comment that should be ignored
                a + b
            }
            use std::collections::HashMap;
        "#;

        let code_b = r#"
            use std::collections::HashMap;
            fn calculate_sum(a: i32, b: i32) -> i32 {
                a + b
            }
        "#;

        let hash_a = CanonicalASTHasher::hash_source(code_a).unwrap();
        let hash_b = CanonicalASTHasher::hash_source(code_b).unwrap();

        // The hashes must match perfectly despite the comment, whitespace, and item order differences.
        assert_eq!(hash_a, hash_b);
    }
}
