### Operational Invariant III: Hardware-Aware Memory Pinning & NUMA Topology

When deploying multi-agent swarms across multi-socket server platforms, memory access latency can degrade by an order of magnitude if threads traverse Non-Uniform Memory Access (NUMA) boundaries. If an agent executing on Socket 0 attempts to read state variables allocated in physical memory associated with Socket 1, the transaction must traverse the interconnect (e.g., Intel UPI or AMD Infinity Fabric), introducing severe latency penalties.

```
+----------------------------------------+       +----------------------------------------+
|              NUMA NODE 0               |       |              NUMA NODE 1               |
|                                        |       |                                        |
|  +------------------+                  |       |                  +------------------+  |
|  | Agent Core 0     |                  |       |                  | Agent Core 32    |  |
|  | (CPU Pinning)    |                  |       |                  | (CPU Pinning)    |  |
|  +--------+---------+                  | Inter |                  +--------+---------+  |
|           | Low-Latency                | connect                  | Low-Latency         |
|           | Local Bus                  | (UPI/ |                  | Local Bus           |
|           v                            |  IF)  |                  v                     |
|  +--------+---------+                  | <===> |         +--------+---------+           |
|  | Local Memory     |                  |       |         | Local Memory     |           |
|  | (Ring Buffer)    |                  |       |         | (Ring Buffer)    |           |
|  +------------------+                  |       |         +------------------+           |
+----------------------------------------+       +----------------------------------------+
```

Architects must enforce three hardware-level constraints:
1. **CPU Core Pinning:** Bind each agent thread to a specific logical CPU core using OS-level system calls (e.g., `pthread_setaffinity_np` in Linux) to maximize L1/L2 cache locality.
2. **NUMA-Local Allocation:** Ensure that the lock-free ring buffers and state fabrics are allocated on the same NUMA node as the executing agent threads using `numa_alloc_onnode` or equivalent kernel-bypass allocators.
3. **Hugepage Backing:** Back the shared memory state segments with 2MB or 1GB Hugepages to minimize Translation Lookaside Buffer (TLB) misses during high-frequency state lookups.

