### Section 2: Low-Level Mechanics & Memory Layout

To achieve sub-millisecond deterministic execution, the runtime must enforce a zero-allocation invariant on the critical path. This requires pre-allocating all memory structures during the initialization phase and laying out data structures to match the micro-architecture of the target processor.

#### Cache-Line Alignment and False Sharing Mitigation

Modern CPUs load data from main memory into caches in blocks called cache lines (typically 64 bytes). If two threads on different cores modify adjacent variables residing on the same cache line, the hardware cache-coherence protocol (e.g., MESI) invalidates the cache line across cores. This phenomenon, known as **false sharing**, degrades performance. 

Our architecture mitigates false sharing by enforcing strict 64-byte alignment on all state-ingestion buffers and thread-local structures using Rust's `#[repr(align(64))]` attribute. Additionally, we pad variables to ensure that read-heavy structures (like model weights) and write-heavy structures (like incoming sensor telemetry) never occupy the same cache line.

```
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+
|                           64-Byte Cache Line                          |
+-----------------------------------+-----------------------------------+
|  State Vector Telemetry (32B)    |  Padding & Alignment Bytes (32B)  |
|  [X, Y, Z, VX, VY, VZ, Q0..Q3]    |  [0x00, 0x00, ... 0x00]           |
+-----------------------------------+-----------------------------------+
```

#### Zero-Copy Memory-Mapped Weight Tensors

Instead of parsing model weights from a file format (like JSON or Protobuf) at runtime, weights are serialized into a raw binary format and mapped directly into the process's virtual address space using the `mmap` system call [1]. By utilizing `MAP_HUGETLB` (Huge Pages), we minimize Translation Lookaside Buffer (TLB) misses, ensuring that address translation for large weight matrices remains highly efficient.

```
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+
|                          Virtual Address Space                        |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+
|  [Page Table] -> Points to Huge Pages (2MB / 1GB)                     |
|  [mmap Region] -> Directly mapped to physical flash/DRAM containing  |
|                   pre-compiled, aligned float32 weight matrices.      |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+
```

This memory-mapped region is cast directly to a static reference of float slices (`&'static [f32]`), achieving zero-copy loading. The CPU can stream these weights directly into SIMD registers without copying them through intermediate user-space buffers.

