# Architecting Heterogeneous AI Inference Fabrics: Deterministic Memory Orchestration for Apple Silicon M-Series Clusters

## Section 1: Architectural Motivation & Scalability Cliff
As enterprise-scale AI inference shifts toward decentralized edge-compute nodes, the reliance on traditional discrete GPU architectures introduces prohibitive latency and thermal overheads. The emergence of M-series "Ultra" class silicon—leveraging Unified Memory Architecture (UMA)—presents a compelling alternative for high-throughput, low-latency inference. However, the move from single-node local inference to multi-node fabric-attached clusters creates a significant "scalability cliff." 

Standard PCIe-based fabric interconnects introduce non-deterministic memory access patterns when traversing the CPU-GPU-NIC boundary. To achieve deterministic inference throughput, we must architect a fabric that treats the collective memory of the M-series cluster as a singular, cache-coherent domain. This requires overcoming the inherent limitations of the memory controller's address-space isolation.

## Section 2: Component Topology & Invariants
To maintain deterministic latency, we propose a "Fabric-Attached Memory Ring" (FAMR) topology. This architecture bypasses standard kernel-space networking for intra-cluster communication, utilizing a custom user-space RDMA shim over a high-speed fabric.

