|                                       - Stalls: Zero-Credit Spin / Epoll                              |
|                                       - Thermal Throttling: DVFS Core Shifting                        |
+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
```

### 5.1 Deterministic Credit-Based Flow Control & Memory Stalling Mitigation

In a zero-copy distributed Unified Memory Architecture (UMA), downstream nodes do not possess dedicated discrete VRAM pools that can be asynchronously overcommitted via conventional paging daemons. When an upstream Apple Silicon node dispatches intermediate activation tensors across the fabric via PCIe Gen4/Thunderbolt Direct Memory Access (DMA), buffer overruns instantly trigger hardware-level bus serialization, stalling the Apple Fabric Interconnect (AFI) crossbar. This ripples backward, blocking both Apple Matrix Execution (AMX) coprocessor units and the Unified Memory System-Level Cache (SLC).

To eliminate memory stalling and guarantee deterministic execution latency under maximum batch saturation, our data-plane incorporates a lock-free, cache-aligned ring-buffer protocol operating directly over mapped Mach physical memory ranges (`vm_allocate` with `VM_FLAGS_FIXED`). 

```c
// Struct layout strictly aligned to 128-byte boundary matching Apple Silicon cache line metrics
typedef struct __attribute__((aligned(128))) fabric_credit_channel {
    _Atomic(uint64_t) tail_sequence;       // Consumer sequence index (cacheline 0)
    uint8_t           _pad0[120];
    _Atomic(uint64_t) head_sequence;       // Producer sequence index (cacheline 1)
    uint8_t           _pad1[120];
    _Atomic(uint32_t) available_credits;   // Available processing frames (cacheline 2)
    uint32_t          credit_watermark;    // Low-watermark threshold for backpressure
    uint8_t           _pad2[120];
    void*             base_dma_va;         // Shared Virtual Address window (cacheline 3)
} fabric_credit_channel_t;

int fabric_reserve_slot(fabric_credit_channel_t *channel, uint32_t required_bytes) {
    uint32_t current_credits = atomic_load_explicit(&channel->available_credits, memory_order_relaxed);

    // Evaluate downstream receiver capacity before staging high-bandwidth tensor transactions
    while (current_credits == 0) {
        // High-watermark breach: execute bounded PAUSE instructions to relax Apple Silicon core cluster
        for (int i = 0; i < 32; ++i) {
            __asm__ volatile("yield" ::: "memory");
        }
        current_credits = atomic_load_explicit(&channel->available_credits, memory_order_acquire);
    }

    // Decrement credits atomically using acquire-release semantics
    while (!atomic_compare_exchange_weak_explicit(
        &channel->available_credits,
        &current_credits,
        current_credits - 1,
        memory_order_acq_rel,
        memory_order_acquire)) {

        if (current_credits == 0) {
            return -EAGAIN; // Fabric queue saturated; trigger upstream AMX compute suspension
        }
    }

    return 0; // Credit secured; node possesses non-blocking DMA execution rights
}
```

### 5.2 Chaos Injection: Dynamic Voltage and Frequency Scaling (DVFS) & Thermal Shifting

Under sustained peak load conditions across 128+ unified memory execution cores, localized thermal throttling can induce uncoordinated dynamic frequency step-downs (DVFS) on single nodes within the fabric. This creates a severe pipeline straggler problem: if Node $N$ undergoes a core frequency drop from 4.05 GHz to 2.4 GHz while handling an intermediate Multi-Head Attention (MHA) split, upstream and downstream nodes in the pipeline stall waiting for intermediate activation synchronizations.

To survive this failure mode without stalling end-to-end inference passes, the fabric control plane runs an asynchronous observability loop that queries the dynamic thermal and performance metrics via I/O Kit registry hooks (`IOServiceGetMatchingServices` targeting `IOPlatformPluginFamily` and `AppleARMIODevice`). 

```c
#include <IOKit/IOKitLib.h>
#include <mach/mach.h>

void monitor_thermal_state_and_rebalance(fabric_credit_channel_t *channel) {
    io_service_t service = IOServiceGetMatchingService(
        kIOMainPortDefault, 
        IOServiceMatching("IOPlatformDevice")
    );

    if (!service) return;

    CFTypeRef thermal_prop = IORegistryEntryCreateCFProperty(
        service, 
        CFSTR("thermal-status"), 
        kCFAllocatorDefault, 
        0
    );

    if (thermal_prop) {
        int64_t thermal_level = 0;
        CFNumberGetValue((CFNumberRef)thermal_prop, kCFNumberSInt64Type, &thermal_level);
        CFRelease(thermal_prop);

        // Thermal Level 2 indicates thermal throttling is imminent or active
        if (thermal_level >= 2) {
            // Signal orchestrator to shrink context chunk sizes & divert speculative draft heads
            atomic_store_explicit(&channel->available_credits, channel->credit_watermark / 2, memory_order_release);

            // Relinquish real-time thread constraint to grant thermal recovery cycles
            struct thread_extended_policy extpolicy = { .timeshare = 1 };
            thread_policy_set(
                pthread_mach_thread_np(pthread_self()),
                THREAD_EXTENDED_POLICY,
                (thread_policy_t)&extpolicy,
                THREAD_EXTENDED_POLICY_COUNT
            );
        }
    }
    IOObjectRelease(service);
}
```

### 5.3 Low-Latency Control Plane Failover and Distributed State Rollback

When intermediate PCIe or Thunderbolt transport links drop or experience transient unrecoverable bit-errors, the memory fabric must isolate corrupted KV-cache allocations within bounded microsecond deadlines. Traditional Raft or Paxos implementations operating over TCP stacks are fundamentally non-viable due to their millisecond-order consensus epochs, which would allow hundreds of megabytes of corrupt pipeline activations to cascade through the cluster.

Our control plane integrates a user-space, zero-copy UDP heartbeat monitor powered by Mach messaging primitives (`mach_msg_send`), linked to a distributed dual-ring ledger:

1. **Active Lease Ring:** Every worker node must refresh a monotonically increasing 64-bit sequence counter every $25\,\mu\text{s}$.
2. **Deterministic Epoch Eviction:** If three sequential heartbeat pulses are missed ($75\,\mu\text{s}$ timeout), the primary node dispatches an explicit `MACH_SEND_MSG` hardware interrupt to the local AMX engine, halting writebacks.
3. **KV-Cache Rollback Protocol:** All dependent KV allocations instantiated past the last validated hardware fence sequence are revoked by sliding the allocation ring head back to the last atomic checkpoint, instantaneously transitioning subsequent requests to a cold-standby node without reallocating base system memory.

