# RFC-0214: Zero-Copy SIMD-Accelerated HTML-to-Markdown Ingestion Engine for LLM Context Pipelines

**Author:** LoLaBo Autonomous Systems Architecture Group  
**Status:** Standards Track (RFC-0214)  
**Date:** October 2026  

---

## Section 1: RFC Scope, Terminology & Guarantees

### 1.1 Scope & Problem Statement
Modern Large Language Model (LLM) agent architectures and Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) pipelines ingest millions of web documents per hour. Standard practices feed raw, unparsed HTML markup directly into context windows or rely on document object model (DOM) tree building parsers written in scripting languages [1, 2]. 

Raw HTML ingestion introduces critical architectural inefficiencies:
1. **Token Inefficiency:** Syntactic HTML markup (`<div>`, `class="..."`, Inline CSS/SVG, script blocks) accounts for 60% to 82% of ingested byte volume, directly inflating LLM context consumption and inference cost per request [2].
2. **DOM Allocation Overhead:** Standard DOM parsers construct dynamic heap-allocated pointer structures for every single node in the document hierarchy. A single 200KB web page can trigger upwards of 10,000 distinct heap allocations, inducing Severe Garbage Collection (GC) pressure or Memory Allocator lock contention in multi-threaded ingestion workers [3].
3. **Parsing Latency Cliffs:** DOM tree construction mandates recursive tree-traversal routines with high cache-miss rates ($L_3$ cache miss ratios exceeding 35%).

RFC-0214 specifies a zero-copy, single-pass HTML-to-Markdown transformation engine that leverages SIMD (Single Instruction, Multiple Data) vector intrinsics (`AVX2` and `NEON`) to scan HTML byte streams at bus speed (>1.8 GB/s per core). The engine operates strictly on borrowed byte slices (`&'a [u8]`), outputting semantic Markdown stream fragments without allocating node objects on the heap.

### 1.2 Terminology
* **Zero-Copy Slice Borrowing:** The allocation-free referencing of underlying input byte buffers using lifetime-bound slice pointers.
* **Vectorized Byte Search:** Utilizing 256-bit vector registers to search for structural delimiter bytes (`<`, `>`, `&`, `\
`, `\\r`, ` `) across 32-byte memory chunks in a single CPU instruction cycle [1].
* **Discard Tags:** Non-semantic structural or executable tags (`<script>`, `<style>`, `<svg>`, `<noscript>`, `<head>`, `<iframe>`) whose entire child subtrees are stripped at the hardware level.
* **Block Elements:** Tags that enforce line-break invariants and Markdown block structural formatting (`<h1>`–`<h6>`, `<p>`, `<ul>`, `<ol>`, `<li>`, `<pre>`, `<code>`, `<blockquote>`).

### 1.3 Deterministic Guarantees & Empirical Performance Matrix
RFC-0214 provides the following formal runtime invariants:
1. **Linear Time Complexity:** Processing duration is bounded strictly to $O(N)$, where $N$ is document length in bytes. Zero backtracking or speculative sub-parsing is permitted.
2. **Zero-Allocation Execution Path:** Under nominal execution (without entity unescaping expansions), heap allocations per document are guaranteed to be zero ($0$).
3. **Deterministic Memory Footprint:** Fixed-size parsing state machines require a fixed stack space of exactly 128 bytes regardless of DOM depth.

