## 1. RFC Scope, Terminology & Guarantees

### 1.1 Scope
This specification defines **RFC-0142**, a zero-knowledge capability attestation and leader election protocol engineered for high-throughput, decentralized agent swarms. In multi-tenant autonomous agent clusters, individual agents must periodically prove authorization to execute privileged tool calls, mutate shared state vectors, or join consensus committees. Traditional authentication mechanisms—such as Bearer Tokens, mTLS X.509 client certificates, or signed JSON Web Tokens (JWTs)—introduce deterministic identity tracking, cross-agent correlation attacks, and centralized verification bottlenecks [1].

RFC-0142 eliminates identity correlation by decoupling capability verification from agent public key identities. Agents maintain membership in cryptographic sets managed via **BLS12-381 Dynamic Bilinear Accumulators** [2]. Capabilities are attested in zero-knowledge using short non-interactive proofs combined with **Verifiable Random Functions (VRFs)** over ring topology for deterministic, unbiasable leader election [3].

```
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|                              AGENT SWARM NETWORK                                  |
|                                                                                   |
|   +------------------+     Zero-Knowledge Proof     +-------------------------+   |
|   |  Agent Node A    | ---------------------------> | Co-ordinating Validator |   |
|   | (Prover Context) |  [BLS12-381 Accumulator]   | (Verifier Engine)       |   |
|   +------------------+  [Ring-VRF Output Seed ]   +-------------------------+   |
|            |                                                    |                 |
|            v                                                    v                 |
|   +---------------------------------------------------------------------------+   |
|   |                  Zero-Copy Shared Memory Frame Buffer                     |   |
|   +---------------------------------------------------------------------------+   |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
```

### 1.2 Terminology
The key words "MUST", "MUST NOT", "REQUIRED", "SHALL", "SHALL NOT", "SHOULD", "SHOULD NOT", "RECOMMENDED", "MAY", and "OPTIONAL" in this document are to be interpreted as described in BCP 14 (RFC 2119).

- **Accumulator Root ($V$)**: A single element in the multiplicative group $\\mathbb{G}_2$ representing the cryptographic commitment of all valid active agent dynamic identity digests.
- **Accumulator Witness ($\\hat{w}_i$)**: An element in $\\mathbb{G}_1$ proving that agent $i$'s capability tuple $y_i$ is a member of accumulator $V$.
- **Capability Bitmask ($C$)**: A 64-bit vector representing atomic execution permissions (e.g., Memory Read, Storage Write, Kernel Bypass Tool Execution).
- **Ring-VRF Nonce ($\\eta$)**: A deterministic pseudo-random proof value computed over the current consensus epoch sequence $E_k$, enforcing unbiasable leader selection without revealing node key identities.
- **Epoch Sequence ($E_k$)**: Monotonically increasing 64-bit integer representing the active 100ms cluster coordination tick.

### 1.3 Guarantees & Invariants
1. **Zero Linkability (Anonymity)**: Given two valid attestation proofs $\\pi_A$ and $\\pi_B$ emitted at epochs $E_k$ and $E_{k+n}$, an observer cannot determine whether $\\pi_A$ and $\\pi_B$ originated from the same agent node with probability greater than $\\frac{1}{N} + \\epsilon$, where $N$ is the accumulator ring size [4].
2. **Unforgeability**: No polynomial-time adversary can construct a valid witness $\\hat{w}^*$ for an unassigned capability bitmask without solving $q$-Strong Diffie-Hellman ($q$-SDH) on curve BLS12-381.
3. **Constant-Time Verification**: Verification of accumulator membership and Ring-VRF evaluation MUST execute within $O(1)$ scalar multiplications and paired pairings, bounding execution latency under $850\\,\\mu\\text{s}$ on standard x86_64 server hardware.
4. **Zero-Copy Transport Integrity**: Wire representations MUST fit within fixed memory boundary limits with explicit C-struct alignments to prevent parsing heap allocations.

