## Key Takeaways & Summary for Systems Architects

### 1. Mathematical Guarantee of $O(1)$ Membership Verification at Scale
Traditional identity-based access control models scale linearly ($O(N)$) or logarithmically ($O(\log N)$) with respect to swarm population size. By leveraging bilinear pairings over the BLS12-381 elliptic curve pairing-friendly curve, RFC-0142 achieves constant-time ($O(1)$) dynamic capability attestation verification. Regardless of whether an autonomous agent swarm contains 100 or 1,000,000 instances, the cryptographic witness verification runtime remains invariant, bound strictly by the time required to compute a double-pairing operation via multi-Miller loop execution (typically $\approx 1.12\text{ ms}$ on modern AVX-512 hardware targets). This decoupling of validation cost from overall swarm size is a fundamental operational invariant for sub-second micro-tier agent coordination.

