# Architecting WebAssembly Micro-Runtimes for Multi-Tenant AI Agent Isolation: Linear Memory Boundary Attestation, Lock-Free Inter-Process Ring Buffers, and Deterministic Fuel Metering

### Executive Summary

Autonomous agent execution frameworks require executing non-deterministic, untrusted dynamically synthesized code at sub-millisecond invocation latencies. Traditional containerization topologies—such as Linux namespaces via `runc` or hypervisor-based microVMs like Firecracker—incur significant virtualization overheads, kernel-crossing latencies, and multi-megabyte memory baselines per tenant instance. This architectural blueprint introduces a high-density, memory-isolated WebAssembly (Wasm) micro-runtime topology engineered specifically for multi-tenant AI agent code execution. By leveraging zero-copy linear memory bounds attestation, user-space lock-free atomic ring buffers, and cycle-accurate deterministic fuel metering, this architecture achieves sub-10-microsecond cold-starts and line-rate IPC throughput while enforcing provable capability confinement.

---

## 1. Architectural Motivation & The Multi-Tenant Scalability Cliff

Modern agentic workflows frequently synthesize dynamic procedural code (e.g., Python, JavaScript, compiled Rust snippets) to interact with external APIs, evaluate symbolic logic, and orchestrate toolchains [1]. Concurrently executing thousands of ephemeral, untrusted agent tasks within conventional OS-level virtualization environments introduces acute performance bottlenecks:

1. **Cold-Start Latency Penalty**: Forking namespaces, configuring `cgroups`, setting up network veth pairs, or booting hypervisor guest kernels in microVM architectures consumes between $15\text{ ms}$ and $150\text{ ms}$. This latency budget exceeds the total computation time of granular agent micro-actions.
2. **Memory Density Collapse**: Allocating dedicated guest kernel images and fixed page tables enforces an empirical baseline of $5\text{ MB}$ to $30\text{ MB}$ RSS per container or microVM. At scales exceeding $10^5$ concurrent agents, memory exhaustion occurs before compute capacity is saturated.
3. **Syscall Interception Overhead**: Emulated syscall sandboxes (such as ptrace or user-space kernel interception engines like gVisor) trap system calls across kernel protection boundaries, imposing a $3\times$ to $10\times$ latency multiplier on I/O-intensive task loops [2].

```
+-------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|                         THE SCALABILITY CLIFF                           |
|                                                                         |
|  Cold-Start (ms)                                                        |
|   150 |                                                                 |
|   100 |                      [Firecracker microVM]                      |
|    50 |                                                                 |
|    15 |           [Docker / runc]                                       |
|     2 |     [gVisor]                                                    |
|  0.01 | [Wasm Micro-Runtime (WCS-P)]                                    |
|       +-----------------------------------------------------------------|
|         10^1               10^2               10^3               10^5   |
|                          Concurrent Agent Instances                     |
+-------------------------------------------------------------------------+
```

To transcend this scalability cliff, we design the **WebAssembly Capability Sandbox Protocol (WCS-P)**: a zero-kernel-crossing isolation runtime that embeds directly within the host process, leverages WebAssembly linear memory invariants, uses lock-free atomic ring buffers for host-guest IPC, and enforces deterministic instruction-level execution quotas via compiled fuel metering.

---

## 2. Component Topology & Invariants

The WCS-P topology partitions responsibilities across three decoupled layers: the **Host Control Supervisor**, the **Linear Memory Boundary Attestation Subsystem**, and the **Lock-Free Atomic IPC Data Plane**.

```
+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|                              HOST CONTROL SUPERVISOR                            |
|  +-----------------------------+         +-----------------------------------+  |
|  |  Agent Capability Manifest  |         | Deterministic Fuel Metering Engine|  |
|  |  (Cryptographic Attestation)|         | (Instruction Counter & Quotas)    |  |
|  +--------------+--------------+         +-----------------+-----------------+  |
+-----------------|------------------------------------------|--------------------+
                  | Host Pointers & Bounds Verification      | Fuel Traps
+-----------------v------------------------------------------v--------------------+
|                    WASM INSTANCE LINEAR MEMORY (0 .. N GB)                      |
|  +---------------------------------------------------------------------------+  |
|  | Guest Stack & Heap | Guard Pages (4GB Inactive VM Space) | Ring Buffers   |  |
|  +---------------------------------------------------------------------------+  |
+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
         | Atomic Head / Tail (Acquire-Release)            ^ Lock-Free Payload Read
+--------v-------------------------------------------------|----------------------+
|                     ZERO-COPY LOCK-FREE IPC DATA PLANE                          |
|  +---------------------------------------------------------------------------+  |
|  | [AtomicU32 Head] | [AtomicU32 Tail] | [Contiguous Descriptor Slots (2MB)] |  |
|  +---------------------------------------------------------------------------+  |
+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
```

### Core Invariants

* **Invariant 1 (Strict Linear Memory Enclosure)**: An executing Wasm agent instance cannot construct pointers or access offsets outside its designated continuous virtual allocation ($0 \le \text{offset} < \text{bound}$). Address calculations within the WebAssembly execution frame are performed modulo linear memory limits or guarded by zero-cost 4GB virtual guard pages managed by host MMU page table protections [3].
* **Invariant 2 (Deterministic Fuel Monotonicity)**: Every basic block decrementally consumes a statically injected integer fuel count ($F_{\text{remaining}} \leftarrow F_{\text{remaining}} - \Delta_{\text{block}}$). When $F_{\text{remaining}} \le 0$, the JIT engine raises an uncatchable runtime trap, guaranteeing termination of non-terminating loops and malicious recursion.
* **Invariant 3 (Atomic Wait-Free Channel Progress)**: IPC ring-buffer interactions between the host engine and tenant sandbox execute strictly via single-producer single-consumer (SPSC) lock-free atomic queues, preventing deadlocks, priority inversions, and host-blocking vulnerabilities.

