## Key Takeaways & Summary for Systems Architects

### Architectural Foundations of Linear Memory Sandboxing and Hardware-Enforced Guard Pages

The core isolation primitive of WebAssembly micro-runtimes relies on contiguous, unmapped virtual memory spaces governed by 64-bit hardware Memory Management Units (MMUs). In a multi-tenant AI agent deployment, software-based bounds checking introduces prohibitive branch-prediction overhead inside inner loops. To eliminate this CPU penalty, micro-runtimes allocate a fixed 4 GiB 32-bit address space wrapped by an unmapped 28 GiB `PROT_NONE` hardware guard region using POSIX `mmap`. 

```
+------------------------------------+----------------------------------------+
|   Wasm Linear Memory (0 - 4 GiB)   |   Guard Page Region (4 GiB - 32 GiB)   |
|   [PROT_READ | PROT_WRITE]          |   [PROT_NONE]                          |
+------------------------------------+----------------------------------------+
 ^                                    ^
 Base Address Pointer                 Out-of-Bounds Access Triggers SIGSEGV
```

Any out-of-bounds array access, pointer arithmetic error, or malicious buffer overflow by an untrusted AI agent naturally falls into this non-addressable guard region. The host kernel traps this access via a zero-cost hardware page fault (`SIGSEGV` or `EXC_BAD_ACCESS`), intercepted by runtime signal handlers and converted into a clean WASM trap. This mechanism guarantees spatial memory safety without injecting dynamic runtime check instructions into JIT-compiled native assembly. 

For state attestation across tenant boundaries, runtimes calculate SHA-256 Merkle root digests over dirty linear memory pages (tracked via Linux `userfaultfd` or kernel soft-dirty bits). This guarantees that host orchestrators can cryptographically verify guest memory integrity before resuming context execution across distributed worker nodes.

