### High-Throughput Lock-Free IPC via Shared-Memory SPSC Ring Buffers

Inter-process communication (IPC) between tenant agents and host AI model orchestrators (e.g., streaming inference tokens or vector store context) cannot tolerate context-switch penalties (POSIX pipes, sockets, or kernel futex contention). Micro-runtimes bridge this host-guest boundary by embedding zero-copy Single-Producer Single-Consumer (SPSC) ring buffers directly within shared sections of the WASM module's linear memory.

```
       Host Writes Tokens (Producer)             Guest Reads Tokens (Consumer)
   ===================================       ===================================
   [ Head Pointer (Cache-Line 0) ]           [ Tail Pointer (Cache-Line 1) ]
                  │                                         │
                  ▼                                         ▼
   +--------------+--------------+--------------+-----------+--+--------------+
   | Message 0    | Message 1    | Message 2    | Unread    |  | Circular     |
   | [Read Only]  | [Read Only]  | [Writing...] | Memory    |  | Buffer Array |
   +--------------+--------------+--------------+-----------+--+--------------+
```

To eliminate CPU cache-line bouncing (false sharing), atomic write (`head`) and read (`tail`) indices are explicitly aligned to 64-byte boundary offsets using explicit cache line padding. Atomic operations utilize strict Acquire-Release hardware memory barriers (`std::sync::atomic::Ordering::Acquire` / `Release` mapping to `ldar`/`stlr` on ARM64 or zero-cost fences on x86-64).

This guarantees sequential consistency for data payloads without taking mutex locks or invoking kernel context switches. Throughput benchmarks demonstrate sustained transfers exceeding $4.8 \times 10^7$ token payloads per second with sub-microsecond end-to-end latency per tenant interface channel.

