### Deterministic Execution Guarantees via JIT Basic Block Fuel Instrumentation

Untrusted AI agents executing generated code introduce severe operational risks: infinite loops, resource consumption attacks, and non-deterministic execution times. Micro-runtimes solve this through compiler-level fuel instrumentation at intermediate representation (IR) compilation stages (e.g., Cranelift or LLVM passes).

```
         +------------------------------------------------+
         | Raw WebAssembly Bytecode / Control Flow Graph  |
         +------------------------------------------------+
                                  │
                                  ▼
         +------------------------------------------------+
         |  Cranelift / LLVM IR Transformation Pass       |
         |  Inject basic-block level decrement & check:   |
         |     fuel = fuel - BLOCK_WEIGHT;                |
         |     if (fuel < 0) trap(OUT_OF_FUEL);           |
         +------------------------------------------------+
                                  │
                                  ▼
         +------------------------------------------------+
         |  Target Assembly (x86_64 / AArch64)            |
         +------------------------------------------------+
```

Prior to JIT compilation, the compiler analyzes every basic block, loop header, and function call site. It calculates the exact compute cost (weight) of the basic block's assembly instructions and prepends an atomic fuel check sequence to the block header:

```rust
// Cranelift IR Fuel Metering Transformation Pattern
fn emit_basic_block_fuel_check(builder: &mut FunctionBuilder, block_weight: u64) {
    let current_fuel = builder.use_var(fuel_var);
    let updated_fuel = builder.ins().isub_imm(current_fuel, block_weight as i64);
    builder.def_var(fuel_var, updated_fuel);

    let is_exhausted = builder.ins().icmp_imm(IntCC::SignedLessThan, updated_fuel, 0);
    builder.ins().brif(is_exhausted, trap_block, &[]);
}
```

If an agent exhausts its assigned fuel allocation mid-execution, the runtime immediately suspends execution, yields CPU execution threads back to the host event loop, and optionally serializes the runtime state vector. This provides deterministic termination, cycle-accurate SLA enforcement, and defense against prompt injection attacks that attempt denial-of-service via computationally expensive algorithms.

