## 2. Component Topology & Invariants

The zero-copy memory-mapped MCP execution runtime divides responsibility between a low-latency Agent Orchestrator process and a sandboxed Tool Execution Kernel. Shared memory spans (`shm_open`/`mmap`) host lock-free ring buffers, eliminating intermediate kernel buffering and data copying.

```
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+ 
|                                USER SPACE ARCHITECTURE                            | 
|                                                                                   | 
|  +--------------------------+                  +-------------------------------+  | 
|  |   Agent Client Runtime   |                  |    Tool Execution Kernel      |  | 
|  |  (Orchestrator Host)     |                  |    (Sandboxed Executor)      |  | 
|  +------------+-------------+                  +---------------+---------------+  | 
|               |                                                |                  | 
|   Producer    | Submit Tool Request              Poll / Execute| Consumer         | 
|   Enqueue     v                                                v Dequeue          | 
|  +-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+  | 
|  |                         SHARED MEMORY IPC REGION                            |  | 
|  |                                                                             |  | 
|  |  +-----------------------------------------------------------------------+  |  | 
|  |  | SUBMISSION RING (SQ) -- Zero-Copy Circular Buffer                     |  |  | 
|  |  | [Slot 0: Enqueue] --> [Slot 1: In-Flight] --> [Slot N: Free]         |  |  | 
|  |  +-----------------------------------------------------------------------+  |  | 
|  |                                                                             |  | 
|  |  +-----------------------------------------------------------------------+  |  | 
|  |  | ATOMIC SLIDING WINDOW RING (64-byte Cache Aligned)                    |  |  | 
|  |  | [Head Seq: AtomicU64] | [Bitmap Array: AtomicU64 x 16]                |  |  | 
|  |  | --> Lock-Free `check_and_set_idempotent` via CAS & fetch_or          |  |  | 
|  |  +-----------------------------------------------------------------------+  |  | 
|  |                                                                             |  | 
|  |  +-----------------------------------------------------------------------+  |  | 
|  |  | COMPLETION RING (CQ) -- Zero-Copy Response Queue                      |  |  | 
|  |  | [Slot 0: Done] <-- [Slot 1: Returning Payload] <-- [Slot N: Ready]  |  |  | 
|  |  +-----------------------------------------------------------------------+  |  | 
|  +-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+  | 
|                                                                                   | 
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+ 
```

### Core System Invariants

- **Invariant I (Zero-Allocation Data Path)**: Slot memory inside the Submission Ring (SQ) and Completion Ring (CQ) is pre-allocated at initialization. Tool payload framing must fit within fixed-size or multi-page contiguous slot extents without dynamic `malloc`/`free` calls during request transport.
- **Invariant II (Cache Line Isolation)**: Producer head, consumer tail, and atomic idempotency bitsets are padded to 64-byte boundary offsets (`#[repr(align(64))]`) to enforce strict isolation against CPU false sharing across cores [4].
- **Invariant III (Single-Atomic Idempotency Assurance)**: Every inbound payload carrying a sequence ID must undergo a single atomic CAS/fetch-or mutation against the `AtomicSlidingWindowRing`. Duplicate execution requests are rejected within sub-nanosecond windows prior to sandbox execution.

