## 3. Data Plane vs. Control Plane Protocol Dynamics

### Wire Format and Memory Layout

Instead of unstructured text-based JSON-RPC over pipes, the high-performance MCP data plane uses a binary serialization framing system (FlatBuffers or Zero-Copy Cap'n Proto equivalent) direct-mapped onto the shared ring memory slots.

```
+-----------------------+-----------------------+-----------------------+-----------------------+
| Byte 0..7             | Byte 8..15            | Byte 16..23           | Byte 24..31           |
+-----------------------+-----------------------+-----------------------+-----------------------+
| Sequence ID (u64)     | Tool Identifier (u64) | Payload Length (u64)  | Flags & Checksum(u64) |
+-----------------------+-----------------------+-----------------------+-----------------------+
| Offset 32..N                                                                                  |
| Raw Binary Parameter Payload Bytes (Directly mapped to Tool Kernel Sandbox Memory)             |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+ 
```

### Atomic Sliding Window Ring Implementation

Below is the complete, production-grade Rust implementation of the lock-free idempotency filter using cache-aligned atomic structures, atomic bitmap modification via `fetch_or`, and ring sliding window epoch advances via `compare_exchange_weak` [5].

```rust
use std::sync::atomic::{AtomicU64, Ordering};

pub const WINDOW_SIZE: usize = 1024;
pub const BITMAP_WORDS: usize = WINDOW_SIZE / 64;

#[repr(align(64))]
pub struct AtomicSlidingWindowRing {
    head_sequence: AtomicU64,
    bitmap: [AtomicU64; BITMAP_WORDS],
}

impl AtomicSlidingWindowRing {
    pub fn new() -> Self {
        // Initialize bitmap array with zeroed AtomicU64 elements
        let bitmap = unsafe {
            let mut data: std::mem::MaybeUninit<[AtomicU64; BITMAP_WORDS]> = 
                std::mem::MaybeUninit::uninit();
            let ptr = data.as_mut_ptr() as *mut AtomicU64;
            for i in 0..BITMAP_WORDS {
                ptr.add(i).write(AtomicU64::new(0));
            }
            data.assume_init()
        };

        Self {
            head_sequence: AtomicU64::new(0),
            bitmap,
        }
    }

    /// Atomically checks if `sequence_id` has been processed.
    /// If unprocessed, marks it as processed and returns `true` (execution granted).
    /// If duplicate or stale, returns `false` (execution rejected).
    pub fn check_and_set_idempotent(&self, sequence_id: u64) -> bool {
        let mut current_head = self.head_sequence.load(Ordering::Acquire);

        loop {
            // Rejection Path 1: Sequence ID is behind the lower bound of the sliding window
            if sequence_id + (WINDOW_SIZE as u64) < current_head {
                return false;
            }

            // Advance Path: Sequence ID exceeds current head; attempt window advancement
            if sequence_id >= current_head {
                let target_head = sequence_id + 1;
                match self.head_sequence.compare_exchange_weak(
                    current_head,
                    target_head,
                    Ordering::AcqRel,
                    Ordering::Acquire,
                ) {
                    Ok(_) => {
                        // Successfully advanced window head.
                        // Clear obsolete bitmap slots between old head and new head.
                        let advance_delta = target_head - current_head;
                        for i in 0..advance_delta.min(WINDOW_SIZE as u64) {
                            let clear_idx = ((current_head + i) as usize) % BITMAP_WORDS;
                            self.bitmap[clear_idx].store(0, Ordering::Release);
                        }
                        break;
                    }
                    Err(actual_head) => {
                        // CAS race lost; update expected head and retry
                        current_head = actual_head;
                        continue;
                    }
                }
            } else {
                break;
            }
        }

        // Calculate bit offset within the sliding bitmap
        let word_idx = ((sequence_id / 64) as usize) % BITMAP_WORDS;
        let bit_mask = 1u64 << (sequence_id % 64);

        // Atomic fetch_or updates state and returns the pre-mutation bit pattern
        let previous_word = self.bitmap[word_idx].fetch_or(bit_mask, Ordering::AcqRel);

        // If the target bit was previously 0, this invocation is unique and valid
        (previous_word & bit_mask) == 0
    }
}
```

